---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/data';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const launchedOn = site.launched ? new Date(site.launched).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' }) : '';
---
<Base title="How this guide works" description={`How ${site.name} stays current: dated facts, visible corrections and a human-reviewed monthly update.`} nav="about">
  <section class="band"><div class="page-text">
    <header class="page-head"><h1>How this guide works</h1>
    <p class="lede">The explanations of how AI works stay stable. The fast-changing facts around them are checked again every month.</p></header>
    <div class="prose">

    <h2>Dates on fast-changing facts</h2>
    <p>Model releases, benchmark numbers and research results carry a small date that shows when someone last checked them against their sources. A fact that is overdue for a check fades until it has been verified again. All of them, with sources, are on <a href={`${base}/sources/`}>Sources &amp; freshness</a>.</p>

    <h2>Mistakes</h2>
    <p>Every chapter ends with a link to report one. Fixes are listed in the monthly <a href={`${base}/notes/`}>field notes</a>, with what was wrong{launchedOn && <>, for everything since the guide launched on {launchedOn}</>}.</p>

    <h2>The monthly update</h2>
    <p>Each month an AI research agent reads a fixed list of sources and proposes changes as a pull request, each with its sources. A person checks every change before it goes live. The agent can read the web and open pull requests, nothing more: it cannot merge, see secrets or change how the site is built. Reading the web and acting on it is exactly the prompt-injection risk the guide warns about, so it gets no more access than that.</p>

    <h2>What this guide leaves out</h2>
    <p>The guide explains how these systems work, how to use them safely and how to judge claims about them. It takes no position on the political, environmental and social questions around AI: what should be regulated, whether training on other people's work is fair, what AI will do to jobs. Those are questions of values. They matter, and they are worked out in parliaments, courts and public debate. A guide about machinery can't settle them.</p>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>Robert Haist, with AI help for research and drafting. Corrections and suggestions are welcome on <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>. Your progress and settings are stored only in your browser.</p>

    <h2>The name</h2>
    <p>It comes from Alan Turing's 1950 paper "Computing Machinery and Intelligence". The guide covers the machinery, so you can form your own view on the intelligence.</p>
  </div></div></section>
</Base>
